# short-description: MedPlatform A/B layout for EFI/GRUB machines
# long-description:
# The A/B disk for machines that boot through GRUB/EFI: qemux86-64 and generic
# x86 hardware. Two identical rootfs slots (RAUC A/B) plus a persistent data
# partition that no update ever touches.
#
#   +------+-------------+-------------+------------------+
#   | ESP  | med-root-a  | med-root-b  |     med-data     |
#   | boot |  (active)   | (inactive)  | LUKS -> /data    |
#   +------+-------------+-------------+------------------+
#
# GPT partition *names* (--part-name) are what /dev/disk/by-partlabel/ exposes,
# and they are what rauc-conf/system.conf and /etc/crypttab refer to. They are
# stable across machines, which is why nothing above the BSP has to know the
# actual block device names.
#
# Naming and location, both of which changed for a reason. This file was
# meta-med-distro/wic/med-partitions.wks: a generic name, in the OS policy
# layer, hardcoding "loader=grub-efi" and "console=ttyS0,115200". It also
# claimed that vendor BSPs needing TF-A/U-Boot at fixed offsets "ship their own
# WKS_FILE from the machine configuration" - false for meta-st-stm32mp, which
# sets only WKS_FILE_DEPENDS - so this layout won by forfeit on the STM32MP257
# and built an EFI/GRUB ESP for a board with no ESP. Two lessons, both encoded
# in where you are reading this: a disk layout names a bootloader, so it lives
# in meta-med-bsp and not one layer up; and do not assume a vendor BSP declares
# its own disk - check.
#
# The A/B *policy* here - two slots, identically sized, med-data outside them -
# is meta-med-distro's, and is what stays portable. See
# med-partitions-stm32mp2.wks.in for the same policy over a TF-A boot chain.
#
# The bootloader RAUC talks to is declared separately, by MED_BOOTLOADER in the
# KAS project file (see rauc-conf.bbappend). Keep the two in agreement: this
# file used to say EFI/GRUB while rauc-conf/system.conf said "uboot" for the
# same machine, and nothing detected it, because no build reads both - the
# contradiction only surfaced when a device booted and RAUC refused to start.

bootloader --ptable gpt --timeout=3 --append="rootwait console=ttyS0,115200 console=tty0"

# --use-uuid and nofail are both load-bearing. wic writes an /etc/fstab entry
# for any partition with a mountpoint, and without --use-uuid it writes a
# device path derived from --ondisk, which defaults to "sda". QEMU presents the
# disk over virtio as /dev/vda, so the entry read "/dev/sda1 /boot vfat
# defaults" and the mount failed - taking local-fs.target with it and dropping
# the whole boot into emergency mode. A UUID does not care what the disk is
# called; nofail means a boot partition that is missing or unreadable
# degrades the device instead of bricking it, which for an appliance in a
# hospital is the only defensible behaviour.
part /boot --source bootimg-efi --sourceparams="loader=grub-efi" --part-name esp --label boot --fstype=vfat --active --align 1024 --size 64 --use-uuid --fsoptions="defaults,nofail"

# --fixed-size, not --size, and that distinction is the whole point of the note
# below. wic treats --size as a minimum and multiplies it by a 1.3 overhead
# factor on a partition that has a --source, but leaves a sourced-less
# partition at exactly --size. With "--size 1024" on both lines this layout
# produced med-root-a at 1.33 GB and med-root-b at 1.0 GB - asymmetric slots,
# in a file that declares symmetry to be a hard requirement, and nothing
# checked it. --fixed-size makes wic fail the build if the rootfs does not fit
# instead of silently resizing.
part / --source rootfs --part-name med-root-a --label med-root-a --fstype=ext4 --align 1024 --fixed-size 1024 --use-uuid

# Slot B is created empty and identical in size to slot A: RAUC writes the
# incoming bundle here. Same size is a hard requirement, not a convention -
# a bundle built from a larger slot A simply does not fit.
# Current utilisation: the EEG rootfs measures ~609 MiB, so ~60% of a slot.
part --part-name med-root-b --label med-root-b --fstype=ext4 --align 1024 --fixed-size 1024

# LUKS container for patient data, calibration and the sealed journal.
# wic cannot produce a LUKS header, so the partition is created as plain ext4
# and converted on first boot by the provisioning step (cryptsetup luksFormat
# with a device-unique key). Doing it on-device is also the correct answer for
# a real product: a key baked into a factory image is not a key.
part --part-name med-data --label med-data --fstype=ext4 --align 1024 --size 512
